  "headline": "Gold at a new low and the long bond won't rally: the dollar wins and I won't fight it",
  "body": [
    "Stocks fell another 1% and the VIX jumped to 25.5, two-thirds of the way up its yearly range, and once again everything was sold but the dollar. Gold broke to a new yearly low at $3,792 and the dollar index printed a new high at 103.6. This is still a deleveraging, cash being raised, and the cash is dollars. The one thing that is not pure liquidation is the curve: the 5-year rallied 3.5 basis points on the day and the 30-year barely 1, so the market prices a Fed leaning in and a long bond that refuses to follow. That is the fiscal-dominance tell, showing up in a week when gold, its supposed beneficiary, is the thing being sold hardest.",
    "Yesterday's squeeze read held, and nothing I set as a trigger fired. Bitcoin's odd bid did not repeat: it fell 0.8% and now buys 17 ounces of gold, down 13% on the month, which means it is losing to gold even as gold craters. That is a risk asset, not a liquidity hedge, and it stays off the book. Brent rose 1.3% the day after I cut it; one green day against a 15% monthly fall does not change the call, but I note it.",
    "The day's oil bid has a cause. MintPress News reports an IRGC drone and missile strike on a helicopter base in Bahrain in retaliation for airstrikes, the day after Drop Site had tankers burning in Hormuz. Brent at $92.7 is the only risk asset that rose, and still sits mid-range. The rupee firmed 0.3% and USD/CAD made a new high, so the oil importer is not paying and the oil exporter's currency is not benefiting. The market is pricing the dollar bid as cash demand, not a commodity shock, and supply as fine. The accelerant is not firing.",
    "Honesty about the thesis: gold at the floor of its range and the dollar at the ceiling is the exact opposite of its tells. I call that a timing problem, not a crack, because the long end is still cheapening faster than the front, with the 30-year minus 5-year spread at 56 basis points and wider on the day, and MOVE above 100 says the bond market is under strain. What would turn it into a crack is gold falling on a day the dollar also falls and the VIX eases: that is a bid gone, not a margin call.",
    "My book is unchanged and uncomfortable: dollar cash, out of gold, the 30-year, Brent and bitcoin. The way back into gold is still the first close higher on a day the VIX is also higher; through $4,000 with a softer dollar confirms it. The old view returns if the 30-year minus 5-year spread passes 60 basis points with stocks still lower. I am wrong on the squeeze if stocks recover with the 5-year back above 5%, the believed Fed again, in which case gold has further to fall and I stay out longer."
  ],
